s Left of the Social?\n\nBy\n\nGeoff\n\n \n\nEley\n\nand\n\nKeith\n\n \n\nNield\n\n.  \n\nUniversity of Michigan Press\n\n,\n\nAnn Arbor\n\n, 2007. xii +\n\n268\n\npp. ISBN 0-472-06964-0, $24.95.\n\nJoseph Maslen\n\nUniversity of Manchester\n\nSearch for other works by this author on:\n\nOxford Academic\n\nGoogle Scholar\n\nTwentieth Century British History, Volume 20, Issue 4, 2009, Pages 581–582, https://doi.org/10.1093/tcbh/hwp024\n\nPublished:\n\n16 July 2009\n\nDiscover the most relevant content quickly with our AI Discovery Assistant\n\n## Extract\n\nValence is not only the interplay of chemical compounds, but also a French city invaded by Arabs and Visigoths, and it is with reference to capture as well as combination that ‘What Is the Valency of Class Now?’ is the last of the four substantive chapters of this book. In the view of the authors, historians should not only continue to integrate class with gender and race, but also understand its development as a universal map for the past. The book utilizes, and advocates, the sub-discipline of intellectual history. Historians of ideas are praised as the most thoughtful contributors to the debates about social class which punctuated the discipline between 1987 and 1997. The main targets of criticism, meanwhile, are the dogmatists of post-modernism and Marxism among the historians of social realities. Regardless of Eley's career as a historian of Germany at Michigan, the book is interpretable as a British companion to Dennis Dworkin's Class Struggles (2007), narrower and more intensive as a summation of previous interventions by an established duo in British historiography.\n\nIssue Section:\n\nReviews--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGeoff Eley | U-M LSA Germanic Languages and Literatures (https://lsa.umich.edu/german/people/emeritus-faculty/ghe.html)\nciteturn51search9 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; Geoff Eley is the Karl Pohrt Distinguished University Professor of Contemporary History. ...   * A Crooked Line: From Cultural History to the History of Society (Ann Arbor: University of Michigan Press, 2005) ...   * A Crooked Line: From Cultural History to the History of Society (Ann Arbor: University of Michigan Press, 2005)\nFor many years he directed CSST, the Program on the Comparative Studies of Social Transformations. Educated at Balliol College, Oxford, and University of Sussex, he previously taught at University of Cambridge. Geoff is on the Board of the GSA (2012-15) and also on the Editorial Board of the German Studies Review.\n\nFellowships\nGuggenheim, German Marshall, U-M Institute for the Humanities, NEH. U-M awards include UMP Book Prize, Distinguished Graduate Mentoring, Michigan Humanities, Faculty Recognition.\nSelected Publications\n\n  * Nazism as Fascism: Violence, Ideology, and the Ground of Consent in Germany, 1930-1945 (2013)\n  * A Crooked Line: From Cultural History to the History of Society (Ann Arbor: University of Michigan Press, 2005)\n  * After the Nazi Racial State: Democracy and Difference in Germany and Europe, with Rita Chin, Heide Fehrenbach, and Atina Grossmann (AnnArbor: University of Michigan Press, 2009\n  * Forging Democracy: The History of the Left in Europe, 1850-2000 (New York: Oxford University Press, 2002)\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nBook Reviews (https://lh.journals.yorku.ca/index.php/lh/article/download/15064/14111)\nciteturn51search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 18.8 years ago; Geoff Eley, A Crooked Line: From Cultural History to the History ofSociety (Ann Arbor: University of Michigan Press, 2005).In A Crooked Line, Geoff Eley deftly combines intellectual history and memoir to\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMicrosoft Word - Eley CV.doc (https://lsa.umich.edu/content/dam/history-assets/faculty-cvs/Eley%20CV.pdf)\nciteturn51search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4 month